/* Per-element discovery history for lessons/chemistry/periodic-table.html.
 * Entry: Symbol: [year, discoverer, place, lat, lon, approx, before-science history, discovery story]
 * approx=1 means the element was known since antiquity, so the ping marks a famous early
 * source or earliest well-documented use rather than a single "discovery" event. */
window.ELEMENT_HISTORY = {
H:["1766","Henry Cavendish","London, England",51.51,-0.13,0,"Ancient peoples met hydrogen only locked inside water, which Greek thinkers such as Thales and Empedocles counted among the basic substances of the world. Alchemists later noticed an “inflammable air” bubbling off when acids attacked metals; Paracelsus described it in the 1500s and Robert Boyle collected it in 1671 without recognizing it as a distinct element.","Cavendish collected the gas from zinc and acid, measured its very low density, and showed it makes water when burned. Antoine Lavoisier named it hydrogène, “water-former,” in 1783."],
He:["1868","Pierre Janssen & Norman Lockyer","Guntur, India (solar eclipse)",16.31,80.44,0,"Helium has no ancient history on Earth at all: nobody could have known it, because it does not form compounds and makes up almost none of the air we breathe. Every civilization saw the Sun, yet no one knew it held an element that the Earth’s surface would not reveal for another 27 years.","Janssen watched the 1868 solar eclipse from India and saw a bright yellow line no known element explained. Lockyer named it helios after the Sun, making helium the first element found in space before it was found on Earth. William Ramsay isolated it from a uranium mineral in London in 1895."],
Li:["1817","Johan August Arfwedson","Utö island, Sweden",58.96,18.31,0,"Lithium was never isolated in antiquity, but mineral springs rich in lithium salts were prized for centuries by the Greeks and Romans as healing waters, long before anyone knew why they seemed to calm the mind. Its main mineral, petalite, was simply a stone.","Arfwedson analyzed petalite from a mine on the island of Utö and found an unknown alkali that behaved like sodium and potassium. Because it came from stone rather than plant ash, it was named lithos, Greek for “stone.”"],
Be:["1798","Louis-Nicolas Vauquelin","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Beryllium hid inside gemstones for thousands of years. Egyptian workers mined emeralds in the Red Sea hills from about 1500 BCE, and Roman writers such as Pliny noted that beryl and emerald looked like the same stone. Nobody knew the shared ingredient was an unknown metal.","Vauquelin found the same new “earth” in both beryl and emerald in 1798. It was first called glucinium for the sweet taste of its salts, then renamed beryllium after the mineral beryl."],
B:["1808","Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac, Louis Thénard & Humphry Davy","Paris, France (and London)",48.86,2.35,0,"Borax was carried west along the Silk Road from dry lake beds in Tibet for over a thousand years. Goldsmiths used it as a flux, Egyptians used similar salts in mummification, and Chinese potters used it in glazes. Its name comes from the Persian and Arabic buraq.","In 1808 Gay-Lussac and Thénard in Paris, and independently Davy in London, extracted the element from boric acid. Fittingly they named it from borax plus carbon, because the dark powder looked like carbon."],
C:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Chauvet Cave, France",44.39,4.42,1,"Carbon is one of the oldest materials people used: charcoal drawings in Chauvet Cave are roughly 36,000 years old, and soot made ink in Egypt and China. Fire, charcoal for smelting metals, and diamonds traded from India by about the 4th century BCE all rest on carbon even though nobody knew it was one element.","Lavoisier showed in 1772 that burning a diamond produces only carbon dioxide, proving diamond and charcoal are the same element, and he listed carbon as an element in 1789. Its name comes from the Latin carbo, “coal.”"],
N:["1772","Daniel Rutherford","Edinburgh, Scotland",55.95,-3.19,0,"Nitrogen compounds were used long before nitrogen was known. Saltpeter (potassium nitrate) was gathered from cave floors and old walls, and by the 9th century Chinese alchemists mixed it with sulfur and charcoal to invent gunpowder. Egyptians used natron, a sodium salt called “nitrum” by the Greeks.","Rutherford, a student of Joseph Black, removed the oxygen and carbon dioxide from air and showed the leftover gas would not support flame or life, calling it “noxious air.” Lavoisier named it azote, and it later became nitrogen, “nitre-former.”"],
O:["1774","Joseph Priestley (and Carl Scheele)","Calne, England",51.44,-2,0,"Ancient thinkers treated air as an element and fire as a mystery. Around 200 BCE Philo of Byzantium noticed that a burning candle in an upside-down jar uses up part of the air, and Chinese alchemists wrote of a component of air they called yin. No one could isolate it.","Priestley heated red mercury oxide in 1774 and got a gas in which candles burned brilliantly; Scheele had made it earlier in Sweden but published later. Lavoisier explained combustion and named it oxygène, “acid-former,” overturning the phlogiston theory."],
F:["1886","Henri Moissan","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"The mineral fluorite was known from antiquity. Romans carved it into prized “murrhine” vessels, and by 1529 Georgius Agricola described using it as a flux, helping metals melt, which gave the element its name from the Latin fluere, “to flow.” Nobody could tame the gas itself; it was so reactive that several chemists were injured or killed trying.","After decades of failed attempts, Moissan finally isolated fluorine in 1886 by electrolyzing a chilled hydrogen fluoride solution in platinum apparatus, winning the 1906 Nobel Prize."],
Ne:["1898","William Ramsay & Morris Travers","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Neon has no ancient history. It is inert and rare, and nobody living before the late 1800s could have detected it in the air.","Ramsay and Travers chilled liquid air and boiled off its parts one by one. The new gas glowed brilliant red-orange in a discharge tube, and they named it neon, “the new one.”"],
Na:["1807","Humphry Davy","London, England",51.51,-0.14,0,"Sodium was known through its salts for as long as people have had salt. Egyptians used natron to preserve mummies and make glass, and Roman soldiers were paid partly in salt, giving us the word “salary.” Soda ash from plants and lake beds cleaned cloth and made glass across the ancient world.","In 1807 Davy passed electric current through molten caustic soda at the Royal Institution and produced silvery globules that burst into flame on water. The symbol Na comes from natrium, from natron."],
Mg:["1755","Joseph Black","Edinburgh, Scotland",55.95,-3.19,0,"Magnesium salts were used long before the metal was seen. Its name comes from Magnesia, a region of Greece where minerals such as magnesium-rich talc and “magnesia” stones were dug up. In 1618 a farmer in Epsom, England, noticed the bitter water of a spring; the resulting Epsom salts became a famous medicine.","Black showed in 1755 that magnesia alba was a distinct substance, different from lime. Humphry Davy isolated the metal itself by electrolysis in London in 1808."],
Al:["1825","Hans Christian Ørsted","Copenhagen, Denmark",55.68,12.57,0,"Aluminum compounds were used for thousands of years even though the metal was unknown. Egyptians and Babylonians used alum as a mordant to fix dyes in cloth and as an astringent, and Pliny wrote about alumen. Alum from Turkey funded whole trading empires in the Middle Ages.","Ørsted first produced tiny amounts of impure aluminum in 1825 and Friedrich Wöhler improved it in 1827. It was rarer than gold for decades; Napoleon III reportedly served honored guests with aluminum cutlery."],
Si:["1824","Jöns Jacob Berzelius","Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Silicon’s minerals shaped human history: flint tools, obsidian blades, and quartz sand melted into glass in Mesopotamia and Egypt by about 3500 BCE. The name comes from the Latin silex, meaning flint.","Lavoisier suspected silica hid an unknown element in 1787, but Berzelius produced pure amorphous silicon in 1824 by heating potassium with potassium fluorosilicate."],
P:["1669","Hennig Brand","Hamburg, Germany",53.55,9.99,0,"Phosphorus was never seen in antiquity, but people used its sources: bone ash, guano, and stale urine, which Romans even collected to wash clothes. Alchemists hunted for a way to turn urine into gold.","Brand, a German merchant-alchemist, boiled down about 1,200 gallons of urine in 1669 and got a white substance that glowed in the dark. He called it phosphorus, “light-bringer,” making it the first element discovered by a named individual."],
S:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Sicily, Italy (ancient sulfur mines)",37.5,13.8,1,"Sulfur, or brimstone, was known from the earliest civilizations. Egyptians used it to purify and bleach; Homer’s Odyssey has Odysseus fumigating his hall with “sulphur, that cures all pollution;” the Chinese used it in gunpowder. Volcanic vents in Sicily supplied much of the Roman world.","Alchemists thought sulfur was one of the principles of all metals. Lavoisier finally showed in 1777 that it was a true element, not a compound."],
Cl:["1774","Carl Wilhelm Scheele","Köping, Sweden",59.51,16,0,"Chlorine’s common compound, salt, is among the oldest traded goods on Earth. Alchemists made aqua regia, a mix of acids that dissolves gold, without understanding that the yellow-green fumes were a new element.","Scheele mixed hydrochloric acid with pyrolusite in 1774 and released a suffocating green gas but thought it was a compound. In 1810 Davy proved it was an element and named it chloros, Greek for “pale green.”"],
Ar:["1894","Lord Rayleigh & William Ramsay","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Argon makes up about 1% of the air people have always breathed, yet it went unnoticed. It is completely inert and its presence was invisible to every ancient and medieval method.","Rayleigh noticed nitrogen from air was slightly denser than nitrogen made from chemicals. Ramsay hunted down the difference and found a new gas. They named it argon, “the lazy one.”"],
K:["1807","Humphry Davy","London, England",51.51,-0.14,0,"Potassium is named for potash: wood ashes leached in pots, used since antiquity by Egyptians, Romans, and Arabs for soap and glass. People burned plants and used the ashes for centuries without knowing they held a reactive metal.","Davy used electricity on molten potash in 1807 and produced the first metallic globules, which skittered and burst into lilac flame on water. K comes from kalium, from Arabic al-qali, “ashes.”"],
Ca:["1808","Humphry Davy","London, England",51.51,-0.14,0,"Lime, made by roasting limestone, is one of humanity’s oldest chemicals. Lime plaster covered floors and skulls at Jericho about 7000 BCE, and Roman concrete and mortar used it in structures still standing today. The name comes from the Latin calx, “lime.”","Davy isolated calcium in 1808 by electrolyzing a mixture of lime and mercuric oxide, after earlier chemists had suspected lime was a metal oxide."],
Sc:["1879","Lars Fredrik Nilson","Uppsala, Sweden",59.86,17.64,0,"Scandium was unknown to the ancients; its minerals are extremely rare, found chiefly in Scandinavia, and it was invisible to early chemistry.","Nilson found it in the rare minerals euxenite and gadolinite. Per Teodor Cleve soon showed it matched the “eka-boron” Mendeleev had predicted ten years earlier, a triumph for the periodic table. It is named for Scandinavia."],
Ti:["1791","William Gregor","Manaccan, Cornwall, England",50.05,-5.1,0,"Titanium ores such as rutile and ilmenite were in beach sands and stones long before the metal was known, but nobody in antiquity could refine them, since titanium is very hard to smelt.","Gregor, a Cornish clergyman and amateur mineralogist, found a new metal in black sand from a stream in 1791. Martin Klaproth rediscovered it in rutile in 1795 and named it after the Titans of Greek myth."],
V:["1801","Andrés Manuel del Río","Mexico City, Mexico",19.43,-99.13,0,"Vanadium has no known ancient use. It hides in ores such as vanadinite and in some crude oils, and was too rare to draw attention.","Del Río found it in a Mexican lead ore in 1801 and called it erythronium, then was told wrongly it was chromium and withdrew his claim. Nils Gabriel Sefström rediscovered it in 1830 and named it for Vanadis, the Norse goddess of beauty, because of its colorful compounds."],
Cr:["1797","Louis-Nicolas Vauquelin","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Chromium’s bright minerals were prized as pigments: the red mineral crocoite was found in Siberia in 1766. Remarkably, bronze swords in the tomb of China’s first emperor, buried about 210 BCE, carry a thin chromium-rich layer that kept them sharp and bright for over two thousand years.","Vauquelin analyzed Siberian red lead ore in 1797 and isolated a new metal, naming it chromium from the Greek chroma, “color,” because its compounds come in so many hues."],
Mn:["1774","Johan Gottlieb Gahn","Sweden (with Carl Scheele)",59.33,18.07,0,"Manganese dioxide is among the oldest pigments: Ice Age artists painted black with it in caves such as Lascaux and Gargas, over 30,000 years ago. Ancient glassmakers added it to remove green tints, earning it the nickname “glassmaker’s soap,” and Spartan steel may have benefited from it.","Scheele recognized a new element in pyrolusite in 1774, and Gahn isolated the metal the same year by heating it with charcoal."],
Fe:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Hattusa, Anatolia (Hittite Empire, Turkey)",40.02,34.61,1,"Meteoritic iron was made into beads in Egypt about 3200 BCE, called “metal from heaven.” Smelting iron ore began in Anatolia by around 1500 to 1200 BCE, and the Hittites guarded the craft. The Iron Age that followed remade tools, weapons, and farming everywhere.","Because iron has been known so long, no single discovery date exists. Its symbol Fe comes from the Latin ferrum."],
Co:["1735","Georg Brandt","Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Cobalt made the blue of Egyptian glass around 1500 BCE and of Persian and Chinese ceramics, long before anyone knew what made the color. German silver miners cursed the useless ore that spoiled their metal, blaming a mischievous goblin, a Kobold.","Brandt showed in 1735 that the blue came from a new metal, not from bismuth as people believed, making cobalt the first metal discovered in modern times whose discoverer is known."],
Ni:["1751","Axel Fredrik Cronstedt","Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Nickel-copper alloys appear in ancient coins minted in Bactria (now Afghanistan) about 235 BCE, and Chinese “paktong,” a silvery nickel-copper alloy, was traded from the 3rd century BCE. German miners called the red ore Kupfernickel, “devil’s copper,” because it yielded no copper.","Cronstedt tried to extract copper from it in 1751 and found a new metal instead, which he named after the goblin (Nickel) of German folklore."],
Cu:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Çayönü, Anatolia (Turkey)",38.21,39.73,1,"Copper was one of the first metals humans worked: hammered native copper beads date to about 8000 BCE in Anatolia. Smelting began by about 5000 BCE, and mixing copper with tin gave the Bronze Age. The Romans called it aes Cyprium, “metal of Cyprus,” which became cuprum.","Because copper predates writing, no discoverer exists. Its long history is why it is both an ancient tool metal and the heart of modern electrical wiring."],
Zn:["1746","Andreas Marggraf (ancient Indian smelting earlier)","Zawar, India (and Berlin, Germany)",24.35,73.73,1,"Zinc ores were used to make brass in Roman times, and by the 12th century Indian smelters at Zawar were distilling pure zinc metal in clay retorts, centuries before Europe. The Romans called zinc ore cadmia.","Marggraf in Berlin published a clear method for isolating zinc in 1746, and the metal was soon produced in Europe. Its name likely comes from the German Zinke, “prong,” for its jagged crystals."],
Ga:["1875","Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Gallium has no ancient history, since it occurs only in trace amounts in zinc and aluminum ores.","Lecoq spotted two violet spectral lines in a zinc-ore sample in 1875. It matched Mendeleev’s prediction for “eka-aluminum,” which Mendeleev had described in detail. It is named for Gallia, Latin for France, and perhaps a pun on Lecoq (le coq, the rooster)."],
Ge:["1886","Clemens Winkler","Freiberg, Germany",50.91,13.34,0,"Germanium was invisible to the ancient world; it appears only as a trace in silver-lead ores.","Winkler found it in a new silver mineral, argyrodite, in 1886. It matched Mendeleev’s predicted “eka-silicon” almost perfectly, and the discovery helped convince skeptics that the periodic table worked. Winkler named it for his homeland, Germany."],
As:["~1250","Albertus Magnus (ancient compounds much earlier)","Cologne, Germany",50.94,6.96,0,"Arsenic compounds are ancient. Yellow orpiment and red realgar were pigments and medicines in Egypt, China, and Rome; arsenic-rich bronze appears from about 3000 BCE, and Roman poisoners made arsenic a legend. The name comes from the Greek arsenikon, “potent.”","Albertus Magnus is usually credited with isolating the element around 1250. Johann Schröder published two ways to prepare it in 1649."],
Se:["1817","Jöns Jacob Berzelius & Johan Gahn","Gripsholm, Sweden",59.25,17.22,0,"Selenium has no ancient use, though its compounds lurk as impurities in sulfur and copper ores.","Berzelius and Gahn noticed a reddish residue in a sulfuric acid plant in 1817. At first they thought it was tellurium, but it was different. They named it selene, for the Moon, since tellurium was named for Earth."],
Br:["1826","Antoine Jérôme Balard","Montpellier, France",43.61,3.88,0,"Bromine gave Phoenician traders their fortune: the royal “Tyrian purple” dye pressed from murex sea snails at Tyre contains bromine in its dye molecule. Ancient peoples wore the color of kings without knowing the element inside.","Balard found a brown-red liquid in salt-marsh brine in 1826, and Carl Löwig had seen it a year earlier. It was named from the Greek bromos, “stench.”"],
Kr:["1898","William Ramsay & Morris Travers","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Krypton is a trace gas in air with no ancient use.","Ramsay and Travers found it in the residue left after liquid air evaporated, and named it kryptos, “hidden.”"],
Rb:["1861","Robert Bunsen & Gustav Kirchhoff","Heidelberg, Germany",49.4,8.69,0,"Rubidium is a rare alkali metal that ancient peoples never encountered on its own.","Using a new instrument, the spectroscope, Bunsen and Kirchhoff found two deep-red lines in the mineral lepidolite in 1861. They named it rubidus, Latin for “deep red.”"],
Sr:["1790","Adair Crawford","Strontian, Scotland",56.71,-5.56,0,"Strontium minerals were long confused with barium carbonates. There is no known ancient use, though its red flame color now lights fireworks and signal flares.","Crawford studied a strange mineral from lead mines near the village of Strontian in 1790. Humphry Davy isolated the metal in 1808."],
Y:["1794","Johan Gadolin","Ytterby, Sweden (analyzed in Turku, Finland)",59.43,18.35,0,"Yttrium had no ancient use. Its ore was an unremarkable black rock until an army officer collected it from a quarry.","Carl Axel Arrhenius found a heavy black mineral in a quarry at Ytterby in 1787, and Gadolin found a new earth in it in 1794. The village of Ytterby ended up giving names to four elements: yttrium, ytterbium, terbium, and erbium."],
Zr:["1789","Martin Heinrich Klaproth","Berlin, Germany (from Sri Lankan zircon)",52.52,13.4,0,"Zircon gems from Sri Lanka and India were traded for over two thousand years, worn as “jargoon” or “hyacinth.” The name may come from Persian zargun, “gold-colored.” People valued the gem but not the metal inside.","Klaproth analyzed zircon from Sri Lanka in 1789 and found a new earth, zirconia. Berzelius isolated the metal in 1824."],
Nb:["1801","Charles Hatchett","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Niobium was unknown in antiquity. Its ore, columbite, sat unrecognized in a British Museum collection for decades.","Hatchett analyzed an American mineral in 1801 and named the new element columbium for Columbia, the U.S. In 1844 Heinrich Rose found it again in Germany and named it niobium, after Niobe, the daughter of Tantalus, because it resembles tantalum."],
Mo:["1778","Carl Wilhelm Scheele","Köping, Sweden",59.51,16,0,"Molybdenum ores looked like lead or graphite, and the Greek word molybdos meant lead. Molybdenite was used as a black marker for centuries. Japanese swordsmiths in the 1300s and 1400s, notably Magoroku Kanemoto, are known to have used molybdenum-bearing steel for their blades.","Scheele showed in 1778 that molybdenite was not graphite or lead but held a new element. Peter Hjelm isolated the metal in 1781."],
Tc:["1937","Carlo Perrier & Emilio Segrè","Palermo, Sicily, Italy",38.12,13.36,0,"Technetium has no ancient history, since almost none exists on Earth: every isotope is radioactive and decays away. Mendeleev left a gap for it as “eka-manganese,” and several chemists falsely claimed to find it, including “masurium” in 1925.","In 1937 Lawrence’s cyclotron in Berkeley bombarded a molybdenum foil and mailed it to Segrè, who worked with Perrier in Palermo. They proved it held a new radioactive element: the first artificially made element. Named technetos, “artificial.” It later became a key medical imaging tracer."],
Ru:["1844","Karl Ernst Claus","Kazan, Russia",55.79,49.11,0,"Ruthenium was hidden inside platinum ore, which reached Europe from South America and later the Ural Mountains. Nobody in antiquity knew it.","Claus isolated it from platinum residues in Kazan in 1844, although Jędrzej Śniadecki claimed a similar find in 1807. He named it for Ruthenia, the Latin name for Russia."],
Rh:["1803","William Hyde Wollaston","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Rhodium is a trace companion of platinum with no ancient use.","Wollaston dissolved crude platinum in aqua regia and found rose-red salts, naming the new element rhodon, Greek for “rose.”"],
Pd:["1803","William Hyde Wollaston","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Palladium was hidden inside platinum ores and had no ancient use.","Wollaston announced it in 1803, first selling it anonymously as “new silver,” which caused controversy. He named it for the asteroid Pallas, discovered in 1802."],
Ag:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Laurion mines, Athens, Greece",37.72,24.05,1,"Silver was extracted from lead ore by about 4000 BCE in Anatolia. The Laurion silver mines near Athens paid for the Greek fleet that won the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE. Silver became coinage, mirrors, and medicine across the Old World; its symbol Ag comes from Latin argentum.","Because it is ancient, no single discoverer is credited."],
Cd:["1817","Friedrich Stromeyer","Göttingen, Germany",51.53,9.94,0,"The Romans’ word cadmia meant zinc ore, used for making brass. Cadmium was hiding in it the whole time.","Stromeyer noticed a zinc carbonate that turned yellow on heating when it should not have in 1817, and traced it to a new metal. The name is from cadmia."],
In:["1863","Ferdinand Reich & Hieronymus Richter","Freiberg, Germany",50.91,13.34,0,"Indium has no ancient use, being a trace metal in zinc ores.","Reich, who was colorblind, asked his assistant Richter to watch for spectral lines in zinc ore. Richter saw a bright indigo-blue line, which gave the element its name."],
Sn:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Kestel-Göltepe mines, Turkey",37.4,34.6,1,"Tin turned copper into bronze about 3000 BCE, launching the Bronze Age. Ancient traders carried it from Anatolia, Central Asia, and Cornwall, and the Phoenicians sailed to Britain for it. The Kestel mine in Turkey was worked as early as about 3250 BCE.","Because tin is ancient, there is no discoverer. Its symbol Sn is from stannum."],
Sb:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Egypt (Nile Valley)",30.04,31.24,1,"Powdered stibnite, called kohl, lined the eyes of Egyptians as makeup and eye medicine from about 3000 BCE, and antimony objects appear in ancient Mesopotamia. The symbol Sb comes from Latin stibium.","Antimony as a distinct metal was described in Europe by Vannoccio Biringuccio in 1540; the alchemical book The Triumphal Chariot of Antimony was published in the early 1600s."],
Te:["1782","Franz-Joseph Müller von Reichenstein","Sibiu, Transylvania (Romania)",45.8,24.15,0,"Tellurium has no ancient use. It occurs in rare gold ores.","Müller found it in gold ores from Transylvanian mines in 1782 and could not identify it. Martin Klaproth confirmed it in 1798 and named it for tellus, Latin for Earth."],
I:["1811","Bernard Courtois","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Seaweed and burnt sponge were used to treat swollen throats, now known to be goiter, in China as early as about 1500 BCE and by the Greeks and Romans. The cure worked because of iodine, which nobody knew existed.","Courtois burned seaweed ash to make saltpeter for Napoleon’s armies. In 1811 too much acid produced beautiful violet vapor. Gay-Lussac named it iodine, from ioeides, “violet.”"],
Xe:["1898","William Ramsay & Morris Travers","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Xenon is an extremely rare gas in air, and had no ancient use.","Ramsay and Travers found it in what was left after liquid krypton evaporated, and named it xenon, “stranger.”"],
Cs:["1860","Robert Bunsen & Gustav Kirchhoff","Heidelberg, Germany",49.4,8.69,0,"Cesium is a rare alkali metal; ancient peoples never found it in pure form, though its minerals are present in some granites.","Bunsen and Kirchhoff saw two bright blue lines in the spectrum of mineral water from Bad Dürkheim in 1860. They named it caesius, “sky blue,” the first element found with a spectroscope."],
Ba:["1774","Carl Wilhelm Scheele","Köping, Sweden",59.51,16,0,"In 1602 a cobbler and alchemist named Vincenzo Casciarolo near Bologna found a heavy stone that glowed in the dark after sunlight, called the Bologna stone. It was barite, and its glow was thought to hold a secret for making gold.","Scheele recognized barium as a new earth in 1774, and Humphry Davy isolated the metal in 1808. The name is from Greek barys, “heavy.”"],
La:["1839","Carl Gustaf Mosander","Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Lanthanum has no ancient use, hidden in rare earth minerals.","Mosander split cerium oxide into parts and found a new element in 1839, naming it lanthanum, “to lie hidden,” since it had hidden inside cerium."],
Ce:["1803","Jöns Jacob Berzelius & Wilhelm Hisinger","Bastnäs mine, Sweden",59.83,15.53,0,"Cerium ores had no ancient use. They were heavy stones from a Swedish copper mine.","Berzelius and Hisinger in Sweden, and independently Klaproth in Germany, found it in 1803. It is named after Ceres, the asteroid discovered in 1801."],
Pr:["1885","Carl Auer von Welsbach","Vienna, Austria",48.21,16.37,0,"Praseodymium had no ancient use. Chemists thought “didymium” was a single element for 40 years.","Auer von Welsbach split didymium into two elements in 1885, praseodymium, “green twin,” and neodymium, “new twin.”"],
Nd:["1885","Carl Auer von Welsbach","Vienna, Austria",48.21,16.37,0,"Neodymium had no ancient use, though it is now the heart of the strongest permanent magnets.","Auer von Welsbach separated it from didymium in 1885 in Vienna, along with praseodymium."],
Pm:["1945","Jacob Marinsky, Lawrence Glendenin & Charles Coryell","Oak Ridge, Tennessee, USA",35.93,-84.31,0,"Promethium has no ancient history because it does not exist in measurable amounts in nature, and false claims of its discovery were made from the 1920s under names such as illinium and florentium.","Marinsky, Glendenin and Coryell separated it from uranium fission products at the Clinton Laboratories in 1945. Named for Prometheus, the Titan who stole fire from the gods."],
Sm:["1879","Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Samarium had no ancient use.","Lecoq found it in the mineral samarskite, named for Russian mining official Vasili Samarsky-Bykhovets, making samarium the first element named after a person, indirectly."],
Eu:["1901","Eugène-Anatole Demarçay","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Europium had no ancient use.","Demarçay found it in samarium samples in 1901 and named it for Europe."],
Gd:["1880","Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac","Geneva, Switzerland",46.2,6.14,0,"Gadolinium had no ancient use.","Marignac spotted the new spectral lines in 1880, and Lecoq de Boisbaudran later isolated it. It is named for the mineral gadolinite and the chemist Johan Gadolin."],
Tb:["1843","Carl Gustaf Mosander","Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Terbium had no ancient use.","Mosander found it while splitting yttria, which came from Ytterby, and named it for that village."],
Dy:["1886","Paul-Émile Lecoq de Boisbaudran","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Dysprosium had no ancient use.","Lecoq needed more than 30 attempts to separate it from holmium, so he named it dysprositos, “hard to get.”"],
Ho:["1878","Marc Delafontaine, Jacques-Louis Soret & Per Teodor Cleve","Geneva, Switzerland and Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Holmium had no ancient use.","Soret and Delafontaine saw its spectrum in Geneva; Cleve separated it in Stockholm in 1878 and named it Holmia for Stockholm."],
Er:["1843","Carl Gustaf Mosander","Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Erbium had no ancient use.","Mosander split yttria into three parts, and erbium is named for Ytterby, the village where the ore was found."],
Tm:["1879","Per Teodor Cleve","Uppsala, Sweden",59.86,17.64,0,"Thulium had no ancient use.","Cleve found it in erbium samples in 1879 and named it for Thule, the ancient name for Scandinavia."],
Yb:["1878","Jean Charles Galissard de Marignac","Geneva, Switzerland",46.2,6.14,0,"Ytterbium had no ancient use.","Marignac separated it from erbium in 1878 and named it for Ytterby."],
Lu:["1907","Georges Urbain","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Lutetium had no ancient use.","Urbain separated it from ytterbium in 1907, and Auer von Welsbach reached the same result at nearly the same time. It is named for Lutetia, the Roman name for Paris."],
Hf:["1923","Dirk Coster & George de Hevesy","Copenhagen, Denmark",55.68,12.57,0,"Hafnium hid in zirconium ores for centuries. It was so similar to zirconium that nobody could tell them apart.","Bohr’s atomic theory predicted an element 72 that acted like zirconium. Coster and Hevesy found it in Norwegian zircon using X-rays in 1923 and named it for Hafnia, Latin for Copenhagen."],
Ta:["1802","Anders Gustaf Ekeberg","Uppsala, Sweden",59.86,17.64,0,"Tantalum had no ancient use.","Ekeberg found it in Scandinavian minerals and named it after the mythical Tantalus, since the oxide would not dissolve in acid, tantalizing him."],
W:["1783","Juan José and Fausto Elhuyar","Bergara, Spain",43.12,-2.41,0,"Saxon tin miners noticed a heavy black mineral, wolframite, that ruined their tin smelting. They called it “wolf’s froth,” Wolfram, saying it devoured the tin like a wolf.","Scheele had shown in 1781 that tungstic acid, from a mineral called tungsten, held a new metal. The Elhuyar brothers produced the metal in Spain in 1783. The name means “heavy stone” in Swedish."],
Re:["1925","Walter Noddack, Ida Tacke & Otto Berg","Berlin, Germany",52.52,13.4,0,"Rhenium had no ancient use. It was the last stable element found.","The Noddacks and Berg detected it by X-ray spectroscopy in 1925 and named it for the Rhine."],
Os:["1803","Smithson Tennant","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Osmium was hidden in platinum ores and had no ancient use.","Tennant found two new metals in the black residue left after dissolving platinum, osmium and iridium. The name comes from osme, “smell,” from its pungent oxide."],
Ir:["1803","Smithson Tennant","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Iridium had no ancient use, though it later became a tracer of the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs.","Tennant found it along with osmium in 1803 and named it for Iris, goddess of the rainbow, because its salts were so colorful."],
Pt:["1748","Antonio de Ulloa (pre-Columbian metalworkers earlier)","La Tolita, Ecuador (and Colombia)",1.03,-79.6,1,"Pre-Columbian metalworkers of La Tolita in what is now Ecuador made platinum-gold jewelry by about 100 BCE, using sintering to work a metal they could not melt. Spanish colonists called it platina, “little silver,” and discarded it as a nuisance in gold mines.","Ulloa reported it in 1748 after an expedition to South America, and William Watson described it to the Royal Society in London in 1750."],
Au:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Varna, Bulgaria",43.2,27.91,1,"The oldest worked gold in the world, dated to about 4600 BCE, comes from a cemetery near Varna on the Black Sea. Egyptian pharaohs, Lydian coins, and Inca temples followed. Alchemists spent centuries trying to make it. Symbol Au comes from aurum, “shining dawn.”","Gold occurs as a native metal, so it was known without smelting, and no discoverer exists."],
Hg:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Almadén, Spain (and Xi’an, China)",38.78,-4.84,1,"Mercury has been mined from cinnabar since antiquity: at Almadén, Spain, since Roman times or earlier, and in China, where the first emperor’s tomb near Xi’an was said to hold rivers of mercury. Egyptians and Greeks used it for medicine, mirrors, and gold amalgam, and alchemists thought it was the base of all metals.","Mercury is ancient, so no one is credited. The symbol Hg comes from hydrargyrum, “liquid silver.”"],
Tl:["1861","William Crookes","London, England",51.52,-0.13,0,"Thallium was unknown in antiquity, and its sulfate later became a notorious poison.","Crookes saw a bright green spectral line in selenium residue in 1861 and named it thallos, “green shoot.” Claude-Auguste Lamy isolated it in France in 1862."],
Pb:["Prehistoric","Known since antiquity","Çatalhöyük, Turkey",37.67,32.83,1,"Lead beads from Çatalhöyük date to about 6500 BCE. Romans used lead for pipes (plumbum, giving us “plumbing”), coins, and cosmetics, and sweet lead sugar wine may have poisoned many. Alchemists thought lead was the oldest of metals, tied to Saturn.","Lead has been known since antiquity, so there is no discoverer. Its symbol Pb comes from plumbum."],
Bi:["1753","Claude François Geoffroy","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Bismuth was known to medieval miners, who confused it with tin or lead. Inca metalworkers at Machu Picchu used a bismuth bronze for knives around 1400 CE.","Geoffroy showed in 1753 that bismuth is a distinct element from lead. Its name may come from German Wismut."],
Po:["1898","Marie & Pierre Curie","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Polonium has no ancient use. Yet the pitchblende from Bohemian silver mines that contained it was known for centuries.","The Curies noticed pitchblende was more radioactive than its uranium could explain, so they processed tons of ore in a leaky shed. Marie named it for her homeland, Poland."],
At:["1940","Dale Corson, Kenneth MacKenzie & Emilio Segrè","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Astatine has no ancient history. It is the rarest natural element, with less than a gram in Earth’s crust at any moment, and Mendeleev left a gap for it beneath iodine.","Corson, MacKenzie and Segrè bombarded bismuth with alpha particles at Berkeley in 1940. Named astatos, “unstable,” since all its isotopes decay quickly."],
Rn:["1900","Friedrich Ernst Dorn","Halle, Germany",51.48,11.97,0,"Radon has no ancient use, though miners in the Erzgebirge suffered a mysterious lung disease, now known as lung cancer from radon, for centuries.","Dorn noticed radium gave off a radioactive gas in 1900. It was named radon from radium."],
Fr:["1939","Marguerite Perey","Curie Institute, Paris, France",48.85,2.34,0,"Francium has no ancient history and is so unstable that fewer than about 30 grams exist in Earth’s crust at any moment. Several chemists thought they had found the missing element below cesium and were wrong.","Marguerite Perey, working at the Curie Institute, spotted it in 1939 as a faint decay product of actinium. It was the last element discovered in nature, and she named it for France."],
Ra:["1898","Marie & Pierre Curie","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Radium has no ancient use. Its ore, pitchblende, was used to color glass yellow in Bohemian mines.","The Curies extracted it from tons of pitchblende in 1898. It glows faintly, so it was used in paint for watch dials with tragic results."],
Ac:["1899","André-Louis Debierne","Paris, France",48.86,2.35,0,"Actinium has no ancient history.","Debierne found it in uranium ore residues in 1899 and named it aktis, “ray.”"],
Th:["1828","Jöns Jacob Berzelius","Stockholm, Sweden",59.33,18.07,0,"Thorium has no ancient use. It is named for Thor, the Norse god of thunder.","A minister, Hans Morten Thrane Esmark, found a heavy black mineral on Løvøya island, Norway, and gave it to his father, who passed it to Berzelius. Berzelius named it thorite in 1828."],
Pa:["1913","Kasimir Fajans & Oswald Göhring","Karlsruhe, Germany",49.01,8.4,0,"Protactinium has no ancient use.","Fajans and Göhring found a short-lived isotope in 1913, and Otto Hahn and Lise Meitner found the long-lived one in Berlin in 1917. The name means “before actinium.”"],
U:["1789","Martin Heinrich Klaproth","Berlin, Germany",52.52,13.4,0,"Uranium in glass is nearly 2000 years old: a Roman mosaic near Naples from about 79 CE contains yellow glass tinted with uranium oxide. Later, silver miners at Joachimsthal in Bohemia dug pitchblende, a dark ore called “bad luck stone.”","Klaproth found a new element in pitchblende in 1789 and named it after the planet Uranus, discovered in 1781. Becquerel discovered its radioactivity in 1896."],
Np:["1940","Edwin McMillan & Philip Abelson","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Neptunium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. Trace amounts form naturally in uranium ore, but far too little for anyone to notice.","McMillan and Abelson bombarded uranium with slow neutrons at Berkeley’s 60-inch cyclotron in 1940. Some uranium-239 decayed into a new element, the first one heavier than uranium. Named for Neptune, the planet after Uranus, as neptunium follows uranium."],
Pu:["1940","Glenn Seaborg and colleagues","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Plutonium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. Only vanishing traces occur in uranium ores, such as at the natural reactors of Oklo, Gabon, about two billion years ago.","Seaborg’s team created it in December 1940 by bombarding uranium with deuterons. The discovery was kept secret during World War II, and plutonium powered the Trinity test and the bomb dropped on Nagasaki. Named for Pluto, the planet after Neptune."],
Am:["1944","Glenn Seaborg and colleagues","Chicago, Illinois, USA",41.79,-87.6,0,"Americium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Seaborg’s group made it in late 1944 in reactors and identified it at the University of Chicago’s wartime Metallurgical Laboratory. Seaborg famously revealed it on a children’s radio show before the official announcement. It is named for the Americas, paralleling europium; today it sits inside ionization smoke detectors."],
Cm:["1944","Glenn Seaborg, Ralph James & Albert Ghiorso","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Curium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Seaborg, James and Ghiorso made it in 1944 by bombarding plutonium with alpha particles at Berkeley. It is named for Marie and Pierre Curie, just as gadolinium honors the chemist Johan Gadolin."],
Bk:["1949","Glenn Seaborg, Stanley Thompson & Albert Ghiorso","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Berkelium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","In December 1949, Berkeley’s cyclotron bombarded americium with alpha particles, and Thompson, Ghiorso and Seaborg spotted a few atoms of a new element. It is named for the city of Berkeley, just as terbium is named for Ytterby."],
Cf:["1950","Glenn Seaborg, Stanley Thompson & Kenneth Street","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Californium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made in 1950 by bombarding curium with alpha particles, with only a few thousand atoms produced at first. It is named for California and the University of California, and is now used as a neutron source."],
Es:["1952","Albert Ghiorso and colleagues","Enewetak Atoll, Marshall Islands (analyzed at Berkeley)",11.5,162.3,0,"Einsteinium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Berkeley scientists found it in the coral dust and debris from Ivy Mike, the first hydrogen bomb explosion at Enewetak Atoll in November 1952, where uranium had absorbed many neutrons at once. The discovery was classified until 1955. Named for Albert Einstein."],
Fm:["1952","Albert Ghiorso and colleagues","Enewetak Atoll, Marshall Islands (analyzed at Berkeley)",11.5,162.3,0,"Fermium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Found alongside einsteinium in the debris of the 1952 Ivy Mike test at Enewetak Atoll, and kept classified for years. It is named for Enrico Fermi, who built the first nuclear reactor."],
Md:["1955","Albert Ghiorso, Glenn Seaborg and colleagues","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Mendelevium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","In 1955 Ghiorso’s team bombarded einsteinium with alpha particles and identified only 17 atoms, one at a time, by watching them decay. It is named for Dmitri Mendeleev, who built the periodic table and predicted undiscovered elements."],
No:["1966","Georgy Flerov and colleagues","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Nobelium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","A team at Stockholm’s Nobel Institute claimed it in 1957, but the result could not be repeated. Flerov’s Dubna group and Ghiorso’s Berkeley group later made real nobelium. IUPAC finally settled the credit and kept the name in honor of Alfred Nobel."],
Lr:["1961","Albert Ghiorso and colleagues","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Lawrencium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Ghiorso’s team made a few atoms at Berkeley in 1961 by bombarding californium with boron. Named for Ernest Lawrence, inventor of the cyclotron that built so many elements."],
Rf:["1964","Georgy Flerov and colleagues (Dubna); Albert Ghiorso (Berkeley, 1969)","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Rutherfordium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Dubna reported it in 1964 and Berkeley in 1969, and each group named it differently in a dispute known as the Transfermium Wars. Named for Ernest Rutherford, who discovered the atomic nucleus."],
Db:["1968","Georgy Flerov and colleagues","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Dubnium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made in 1968 at Dubna by firing neon ions at americium, and also claimed at Berkeley in 1970. The name honors Dubna, the Russian research town that hosts the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research."],
Sg:["1974","Albert Ghiorso and colleagues","Berkeley, California, USA",37.87,-122.27,0,"Seaborgium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Ghiorso’s Berkeley team made it in 1974 by bombarding californium with oxygen ions. It was the first element named for a living person, Glenn Seaborg, who saw his name on the table."],
Bh:["1981","Peter Armbruster & Gottfried Münzenberg","Darmstadt, Germany",49.93,8.68,0,"Bohrium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","At GSI in Darmstadt, Armbruster’s team fused chromium and bismuth atoms in 1981 and found a handful of atoms lasting milliseconds. Named for Niels Bohr, who explained the structure of the atom."],
Hs:["1984","Peter Armbruster & Gottfried Münzenberg","Darmstadt, Germany",49.93,8.68,0,"Hassium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made at GSI in 1984 by firing iron ions at lead, detecting only three atoms. Its name comes from Hassia, the Latin name of the German state of Hesse, where GSI is located."],
Mt:["1982","Peter Armbruster & Gottfried Münzenberg","Darmstadt, Germany",49.93,8.68,0,"Meitnerium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Just one atom was detected at GSI in 1982, and a second not until 1988. Named for Lise Meitner, who helped explain nuclear fission but was left out of the Nobel Prize for it."],
Ds:["1994","Sigurd Hofmann and colleagues","Darmstadt, Germany",49.93,8.68,0,"Darmstadtium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Hofmann’s team at GSI made it in 1994 by firing nickel ions at lead, seeing just a single atom. Named for Darmstadt, the German city where it was made."],
Rg:["1994","Sigurd Hofmann and colleagues","Darmstadt, Germany",49.93,8.68,0,"Roentgenium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made at GSI in December 1994 by firing nickel ions at bismuth, and confirmed later. Named for Wilhelm Röntgen, who discovered X-rays in 1895."],
Cn:["1996","Sigurd Hofmann and colleagues","Darmstadt, Germany",49.93,8.68,0,"Copernicium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made at GSI in 1996 by firing zinc ions at lead. Named for Nicolaus Copernicus, who placed the Sun at the center of the solar system; the name was approved in 2010."],
Nh:["2004","Kosuke Morita and colleagues","RIKEN, Wako, Japan",35.78,139.61,0,"Nihonium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Morita’s team at RIKEN fired zinc ions at bismuth and observed a single atom in 2004, with two more in 2005 and 2012. It is the first element discovered in Asia, named for Nihon, the Japanese name for Japan."],
Fl:["1999","Yuri Oganessian and colleagues","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Flerovium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Oganessian’s team at Dubna fired calcium-48 at plutonium in 1999 to make it. It is named for the Flerov Laboratory of Nuclear Reactions and its founder Georgy Flerov, and lies near the predicted “island of stability.”"],
Mc:["2003","Yuri Oganessian and colleagues","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Moscovium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made at Dubna in 2003 by firing calcium-48 at americium, with American collaborators from Livermore. Named for the Moscow region, home of Dubna."],
Lv:["2000","Yuri Oganessian and colleagues","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Livermorium has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made at Dubna in 2000 by firing calcium-48 at curium, in collaboration with Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory in California, which the name honors."],
Ts:["2010","Yuri Oganessian and colleagues","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Tennessine has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made at Dubna in 2010 by firing calcium-48 at berkelium, which was produced at Oak Ridge National Laboratory in Tennessee and shipped to Russia. The name honors Tennessee."],
Og:["2002","Yuri Oganessian and colleagues","Dubna, Russia",56.74,37.19,0,"Oganesson has no ancient history: it is made only in laboratories or reactors, and no natural deposit exists for anyone to have used before scientists built it. It does not exist in nature.","Made at Dubna from calcium-48 and californium in 2002, with only a handful of atoms ever seen. It completed the seventh row of the table in 2016 and is named for Yuri Oganessian, only the second element named for a living person after seaborgium."]
};
